    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d26">

                <pb facs="0121" n="39" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg39"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51 W 89 Czechoslovakia/95: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Czechoslovakia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Einstein</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Prague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-06-17T13:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 17, 1925—1
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 3:15 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>30. Mr. Clarence Dillon now at Prague with Colonel Logan<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d26-f1"/> informs me that he proposes telling Dr. Beneš that after
                    Czechoslovak Government has replied satisfactorily to Government’s recent note
                    regarding refunding of indebtedness to the United States he is disposed <pb
                        facs="0122" n="40" xml:id="pg40"/> tentatively to discuss short-term credit
                    to be established for purpose stabilizing the currency. Mr. Dillon’s intention
                    is to cooperate fully with Department and conform himself to its wishes.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Einstein</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d26-f1" n="5" xml:id="d26-f1-content">
                <p>James A. Logan, Jr., formerly American unofficial representative on the
                    Reparation Commission.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
